{"_data": [{"type": "metadata", "contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": "Vanessa in Cooking School: Indian Food"}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "Out and About"}, {"type": "position", "content": 4}, {"type": "origin", "content": "scholar > Delicious Food > India"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 7}, {"type": "level", "content": "A2.1"}]}, {"type": "achievment_test", "contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}]}, {"type": "objectives", "contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": " Accurately use information about travel and vacations in social situations."}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Identifies general information in short written or spoken conversations."}, {"type": "secondary_2", "content": "Identifies general information in short written or spoken conversations."}, {"type": "secondary_3", "content": "Gives appropriate directions to places taking into account the type of interlocutor."}, {"type": "secondary_4", "content": "Identifies general information in short written or spoken conversations."}, {"type": "secondary_5", "content": "Identifies general information in short written or spoken conversations."}, {"type": "secondary_6", "content": "Identifies general information in short written or spoken conversations."}, {"type": "secondary_7", "content": "Gives appropriate directions to places taking into account the type of interlocutor."}, {"type": "secondary_8", "content": "Identifies general information in short written or spoken conversations."}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "  Intonation with Contrast"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "Key words or words about new information in a sentence are stressed when you speak.  "}, {"type": "text", "content": "When you want to emphasize one word or an idea over another, you change the sound of your voice. These are called <span class =\"strong\">contrast words</span> . You can show emotions or feelings by stressing contrast words."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Example:<br /><br />  Say these two sentences aloud. Stress the bolded words. How does the meaning change?<br /><br />  Sally studies <span class =\"strong\">Spanish</span> . <br/>Sally <span class =\"strong\">studies</span>  Spanish, but she doesn't <span class =\"strong\">use</span>  it."}, {"type": "text", "content": "When you stress the contrast words instead of key words, you can sound angry or frustrated."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Example:<br /><br />   Say these two sentences. Stress the bolded words. Notice that the meaning of the sentence changes when you stress contrast words.<br /><br />   <span class =\"strong\">Sally</span>  said it is good.<br />  John <span class =\"strong\">doesn't</span>   like it.<br />   Where <span class =\"strong\">are</span>  they going tonight?<br />"}, {"type": "text", "content": "When you stress modal verbs, it can have an opposite meaning."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Example:<br /><br />  Peter <span class =\"strong\">would</span>  help us, if he were here.<br />  People <span class =\"strong\">should</span>  exercise more, but many dislike it."}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "church "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a place of worship "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun) "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: We went to church every Sunday."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn4le07ob04aud01"}]}, {"type": "target_word_describer", "contents": [{"type": "text", "content": " These are words you hear in a conversation at a restaurant."}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "burger "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "short form of the word hamburger; a pattie inside a bun "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)  "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: I would like to order a burger and fries."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn4le07ob04aud02"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "purple "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "the color of royalty "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: I really like my purple purse."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn4le07ob04aud03"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "surf and turf "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a mix of meat and seafood  "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: My surf and turf dinner had fish and beef."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn4le07ob04aud04"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "mac and cheese "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a meal of noodles, cheese and a creamy sauce "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun) "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Did you finish all the mac and cheese?"}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn4le07ob04aud05"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "buckle "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a metal or plastic device that is attached to one end of a belt or strap and connected to the other end "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: I like the buckle on your purse."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn4le07ob04aud06"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "to mind "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to care about or worry about (something or someone) "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb) "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Do you mind if I go with you?"}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn4le07ob04aud07"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "oyster "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a shellfish  "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Are you having oysters for dinner?"}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn4